---
// Low-opacity, edge-anchored scene photograph for section backgrounds
// (Canva "Hamdam Website Art Direction", Section Background Rhythm).
// Desktop/tablet only: on narrow mobile viewports there is no room to bleed
// an image from the edge without risking it sitting behind the text column,
// so it is hidden below the sm breakpoint rather than assumed safe.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro:assets';

interface Props {
  image: ImageMetadata;
}

const { image } = Astro.props;
---

{
  /* z-0, not -z-10 (2026-08-14). Every consumer of this component used to sit
     on a translucent wash, so a photograph behind the section still showed
     through it. With the day arc the sections paint opaque gradients, and a
     negative z-index put the photograph behind the very background that is
     now painting -- it rendered nothing at all. It sits above the section
     background and below the content, which is where a section background
     photograph belongs. Consumers must position their content (`relative`),
     or it will paint underneath this. */
}
<div class="scene-bg pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 end-0 z-0 hidden w-[calc(50%-336px)] max-w-3xl overflow-hidden xl:block" aria-hidden="true">
  <Image
    src={image}
    alt=""
    width={image.width}
    height={image.height}
    class="scene-bg__img h-full w-full object-cover"
  />
</div>

<style>
  /* Masked rather than uniformly faded (2026-08-14). At a flat 25% these
     photographs were, measured on the built page, close to invisible: the
     quiet-garden shot behind the companions section read as a faint smudge
     and the dawn shot behind the verses only surfaced at the far right edge.
     They were costing their bytes and delivering nothing, which is the worst
     of both options -- either a photograph is doing emotional work or it
     should not be there.

     So the opacity goes up and a mask takes over the job the opacity was
     badly doing: the image is legible at the outer edge and gone before it
     reaches the text column, which is a stronger guarantee of legibility
     than a global fade ever was, because it is positional rather than
     tonal. Nothing is placed behind the copy at any strength. */
  /* The geometry, not the mask, is what guarantees legibility.
     `w-[calc(50%-336px)]` is derived from the content rather than tuned to a
     viewport: 336px is half of max-w-2xl (672px), the widest centred block
     any consumer places beside this image, so the container occupies exactly
     the gutter outside that column and is clear of it at *every* width by
     construction.

     The previous version got this wrong in a way worth recording. It was
     `w-3/5` with the mask stopping at 44%, a number derived only from the
     1440px case -- and the comment claimed no photograph was ever composited
     under a glyph. That held at 1440 and nowhere else: the container is a
     percentage of the viewport while the content column has a fixed max
     width, so as the viewport narrows the column reaches further into the
     image. Measured against the rendered page, six text elements sat over
     the photograph at 640-1024px and one still did at 1280px. A single-
     viewport derivation should never have been written up as a proof.

     `xl:block` follows from the same arithmetic: below 1280px the gutter is
     under ~300px and not worth a photograph, and at 672px it is nothing at
     all. Losing the image on smaller screens is the right trade -- the skill
     this repo follows asks for photography as an emotional layer with proven
     contrast, not for an image that costs bytes and sits under body copy.

     The mask is now purely aesthetic, softening the image's inner edge
     inside a region that never meets text. */
  .scene-bg__img {
    opacity: 0.5;
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, black 0%, black 55%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to left, black 0%, black 55%, transparent 100%);
  }
  /* The container is anchored with `end-0`, so it sits on the left in RTL
     and the mask has to fade the other way or it would erase the visible
     edge and keep the half nearest the Farsi text column. */
  :global([dir='rtl']) .scene-bg__img {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, black 0%, black 55%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, black 0%, black 55%, transparent 100%);
  }
</style>
